      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we start by determining the GCD and LCM of these two numbers. We can use the relationship between the GCD and LCM of two numbers, which is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(a, b) \\times \\text{LCM}(a, b) = a \\times b\n\\]\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\):\n\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor present in both factorizations. Therefore, the GCD of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the LCM by taking the highest power of each prime factor appearing in the factorizations. Thus, the LCM of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\nNow, we calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\n\\[\n\\text{GCD}(18, 42) \\times \\text{LCM}(18, 42) = 6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{756}\n\\]",
