      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), and then compute their product.\n\nFirst, let's find the prime factorizations of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\):\n\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor present in both numbers. Here, the common prime factors are \\(2\\) and \\(3\\):\n\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the LCM. The LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor present in either number:\n\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\nNow, we compute the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126\n\\]\n\nTo find \\(6 \\times 126\\):\n\n\\[\n6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{756}\n\\]",
